    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests pass, including the new `Cp26Tests` (national-length validation) and the existing `Cp08`/`Cp02`/`Cp03` telephone tests. **BUILD SUCCESS** with 36 tests run, 0 failures.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe requirement: validate an E.164 telephone's national-number length against its country code \u2014 `+61` needs 9 national digits, `+1` needs 10 \u2014 and reject with 400 when wrong.\n\nI added the check inside the shared `toE164` helper in `NormalizeOwnerTelephone.java`, right after the existing 8\u201315 total-digits guard:\n\n```java\n// Country-specific national-number length: '+61' requires 9 national digits,\n// '+1' requires 10. Other country codes keep only the general 8-15 rule above.\nif (digits.startsWith(\"61\")) {\n    if (digits.length() - 2 != 9) {\n        return null;\n    }\n} else if (digits.startsWith(\"1\")) {\n    if (digits.length() - 1 != 10) {\n        return null;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nDesign notes:\n- Putting it in `toE164` keeps normalization and the duplicate-check comparison (`RequireUniqueOwnerTelephone`) on the same canonical rules. Returning `null` makes `NormalizeOwnerTelephone` throw `OwnerTelephoneInvalidException` \u2192 400, exactly as the malformed-number path already does.\n- Only `+61` and `+1` get a specific length; other country codes (e.g. the `+64` case in `Cp08`) keep the general 8\u201315 rule and still pass.\n- I also updated the class Javadoc to document the new rule.\n\nVerified behavior against the tests: `+61 123` \u2192 national `123` (\u2260 9) \u2192 400 (Cp26); `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678` (9 national) still OK (Cp02/Cp08); `+64 21 123 456` unaffected (Cp08); national/international duplicates still collide (Cp03).",
